---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Quickstart — TrustNotch Docs"
  description="Record an entry, fetch its proof, and verify it yourself with an independent open-source tool. Signed receipts, RFC 6962 inclusion proofs, Bitcoin anchoring."
  canonicalPath="/docs"
>
  <div class="page-wrapper">

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="section hero" aria-labelledby="docs-hero-heading">
      <div class="section__label">Quickstart</div>
      <h1 id="docs-hero-heading">From zero to a proof you verified yourself</h1>
      <p class="hero__sub">
        Record an entry, fetch its proof, and check it on your own machine with a tool
        that talks to nothing we run. You don't have to trust us — in about ten minutes
        you can confirm that for yourself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Timing -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="timing-heading">
      <h2 id="timing-heading">When each layer of proof arrives</h2>
      <p class="body-text">
        A proof is built in layers, and they don't all arrive at once. Knowing the
        schedule first means nothing below looks broken when it is working correctly.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrapper mt-sm">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Layer</th>
              <th scope="col">Available</th>
              <th scope="col">What it establishes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Ed25519 receipt</td>
              <td>Immediately</td>
              <td>We saw this exact entry at the stated time</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>RFC 6962 inclusion proof</td>
              <td>Within about 60 seconds</td>
              <td>The entry sits in a Merkle tree it cannot be quietly removed from</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>OpenTimestamps commitment</td>
              <td>With the first proof bundle</td>
              <td>A calendar has committed to the batch root</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Bitcoin attestation</td>
              <td>Later, typically hours</td>
              <td>That commitment is embedded in Bitcoin's history</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        Steps 1 to 4 take you through the inclusion proof. The Bitcoin attestation
        arrives on its own schedule; step 5 covers how to read its status.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1. Key -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="key-heading">
      <h2 id="key-heading">1. Get an API key</h2>
      <p class="body-text">
        Two ways in, depending on whether a person or a program is signing up. Both
        produce the same kind of key, and both show it exactly once.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">If you are a person</h3>
      <p class="body-text">
        Sign up at <a href="https://app.trustnotch.com/signup">app.trustnotch.com/signup</a>,
        confirm your email, then create a key from the API keys panel on your dashboard.
        Hold up to five at a time; revoke any of them whenever you like.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">If you are an agent</h3>
      <p class="body-text">
        Signing up takes two calls and no human. Request a challenge, solve a small
        proof-of-work, submit the solution. The work is plain SHA-256 with nothing
        outside the standard library — it makes bulk account creation expensive, and
        that is all it is for.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`import hashlib, json, urllib.request

BASE = "https://api.trustnotch.com"
SEP = b"|"

def post(path, body=None):
    data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else b""
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        BASE + path, data=data,
        headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"}, method="POST")
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read())

def leading_zero_bits(d):
    bits = 0
    for byte in d:
        if byte == 0:
            bits += 8
            continue
        bits += 8 - byte.bit_length()
        break
    return bits

# Ask for a challenge.
ch = post("/v1/accounts/challenge")

# Find a nonce whose digest has enough leading zero bits.
n = 0
while True:
    nonce = str(n)
    msg = SEP.join((ch["random"].encode(), str(ch["issued_at"]).encode(), nonce.encode()))
    if leading_zero_bits(hashlib.sha256(msg).digest()) >= ch["difficulty_bits"]:
        break
    n += 1

# Submit it. The key is in account["api_key"], shown only here.
account = post("/v1/accounts", {
    "random": ch["random"],
    "issued_at": ch["issued_at"],
    "signature": ch["signature"],
    "nonce": nonce,
})

print(account["account_id"], account["plan"], account["included_monthly"])`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        At the default 20 bits that search takes roughly a million attempts — a second
        or two in Python, less in a compiled language. The challenge lasts 300 seconds
        and is bound to the IP that asked for it, so solve and submit from the same
        host. New accounts start free with 10,000 entries a month and no card.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. Record -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="record-heading">
      <h2 id="record-heading">2. Record an entry</h2>
      <p class="body-text">
        Send the entry with your key as a bearer token. Two submission modes; pick one
        per entry.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">Standard — send the payload</h3>
      <p class="body-text">
        We canonicalize your JSON, hash it, and store both.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`curl -X POST https://api.trustnotch.com/v1/logs -H "Authorization: Bearer $TN_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"payload":{"agent":"support-bot","action":"tool_call","tool":"search"}}'`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`{
  "id": "01a015d7-2d97-74d2-9d02-b268349a9c56",
  "content_hash": "eb3bca757a55f2d2c8640195882504a862e1e11c9f3fb399fce74e2893a825d8",
  "created_at": "2026-08-18T17:06:58.582994Z",
  "receipt_signature": "N7uipFCbADukNcoLCa848V5u6BWC9IH...",
  "receipt_key_id": 1
}`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        <code>receipt_signature</code> is an Ed25519 signature over the entry's leaf
        hash, made with the published key named by <code>receipt_key_id</code>. Step 4
        checks it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">Zero-PII — send only the hash</h3>
      <p class="body-text">
        If the content is personal data, hash it yourself and send the digest alone, so
        we never receive the underlying bytes. Add a random salt per record: a plain hash
        of a short value, like an email address, can be guessed by hashing candidates,
        and the salt stops that. Keep the salt and the original with your own records,
        because you need both to show later what an entry stood for. Deleting them is
        also how you erase: without them, the hash we hold cannot practically be tied to
        anyone. This supports data minimisation under GDPR, and for personal data it is
        usually the mode you want.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`# Hashed locally with a random salt. The bytes never leave your machine.
SALT=$(openssl rand -hex 16)
H=$(printf '%s%s' "$SALT" '{"subject":"alice@example.com","decision":"approved"}' | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
# Keep $SALT and the original record with your own copy of the log.

curl -X POST https://api.trustnotch.com/v1/logs -H "Authorization: Bearer $TN_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "{\\"content_hash\\":\\"$H\\"}"`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        The response has the same shape, and the <code>content_hash</code> it returns
        matches the digest you computed. Receipt, inclusion and anchoring all work
        exactly as they do in standard mode. Send neither field or both and the request
        is rejected.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Proof -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="proof-heading">
      <h2 id="proof-heading">3. Fetch the proof</h2>
      <p class="body-text">
        Ask for the bundle using the <code>id</code> from step 2.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`curl https://api.trustnotch.com/v1/logs/$LOG_ID/proof -H "Authorization: Bearer $TN_KEY" -o bundle.json`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        Try it immediately and you get a 409. That is correct: entries are batched into
        a Merkle tree on a roughly 60-second timer, and there is no inclusion proof
        before the batch exists.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`{"detail":"proof not available yet: entry not batched (retry within ~60s)"}`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        Wait a minute, ask again, and you get the leaf the proof commits to, the signed
        receipt, the inclusion proof, and the anchor state:
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`{
  "version": { "proof_format": 1, "leaf_format": 1 },
  "entry": {
    "id": "01a015d7-2d97-74d2-9d02-b268349a9c56",
    "account_id": "01a015d6-69e4-7100-9731-db878bda2336",
    "content_hash": "eb3bca757a55f2d2c86401958825...",
    "created_at": "2026-08-18T17:06:58.582994Z"
  },
  "leaf_hash": "b78311af2fc8d109113e9996d880653b...",
  "receipt": { "signature": "37bba2a4509b003ba435...", "key_id": 1 },
  "inclusion": {
    "leaf_index": 0,
    "tree_size": 2,
    "merkle_root": "f1d63cfac67f57530ff75fb10c967fb0...",
    "audit_path": ["0b45a2aeda5efacbce02b0f74b0db7cf..."]
  },
  "anchor": {
    "ots_status": "pending",
    "bitcoin_block_height": null,
    "bitcoin_block_hash": null,
    "bitcoin_tx_id": null,
    "confirmations": 0,
    "ots_proof": "004f70656e54696d657374616d7073..."
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        The bundle is self-contained. Everything needed to check it is in the file,
        except the public keys — and those ship inside the verifier rather than being
        fetched from us.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. Verify -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="verify-heading">
      <h2 id="verify-heading">4. Verify it yourself</h2>
      <p class="body-text">
        This is the step that matters. The verifier is a separate open-source package
        carrying its own copy of the trusted key directory.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`pip install trustnotch
trustnotch verify bundle.json`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`receipt:            pass
inclusion:          pass
anchor_structural:  na

anchor:             not yet anchored to Bitcoin (nothing to verify at this tier)

errors:
  - ots_proof commits the root via a calendar attestation but is not yet anchored to a Bitcoin block

verified (anchoring not yet confirmed)`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        Exit code <code>0</code> means no tier failed, and a tier reported
        <code>na</code> is not a failure. <code>1</code> means at least one tier failed.
        <code>2</code> means the file could not be read as a proof bundle. Note the
        <code>errors</code> line on a passing run: it reports that the Bitcoin
        attestation hasn't landed, which for a minutes-old entry is expected, not a fault.
      </p>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        Fetch the bundle again once its batch is in a Bitcoin block, and the anchor tier
        passes too:
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`receipt:            pass
inclusion:          pass
anchor_structural:  pass

anchor:             structurally verified -- asserted block 970063 (extracted from the proof); confirmation depth is not checked by this tool

verified`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        The block height comes from the proof itself, not from our metadata. The verifier
        does not check how deep that block is. Matching it to the real chain is the step
        described in section 5.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">Prove it can say no</h3>
      <p class="body-text">
        A verifier that approves everything is worthless. Change one character of the
        entry's <code>content_hash</code> and run it again:
      </p>

      <div class="code-block mt-sm">
        <pre><code>{`receipt:            fail
inclusion:          fail
anchor_structural:  na

anchor:             not yet anchored to Bitcoin (nothing to verify at this tier)

errors:
  - echoed leaf_hash does not match recomputed value (recomputed used)
  - receipt signature does not verify against the recomputed leaf
  - inclusion proof does not reconstruct the stated merkle_root
  - ots_proof commits the root via a calendar attestation but is not yet anchored to a Bitcoin block

NOT verified (failed: receipt, inclusion)`}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="body-text mt-sm">
        One altered character fails two tiers, the receipt and the inclusion proof, and
        the exit code becomes <code>1</code>. The first error line is the verifier
        noticing that the bundle's own <code>leaf_hash</code> no longer matches the
        content, and recomputing it rather than trusting it. Do this once, yourself,
        before trusting anything else here.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">Verifying without keys</h3>
      <p class="body-text">
        The default run already needs no network: it checks the receipt against the keys
        that ship inside the package. <code>--offline</code> is narrower. It uses no keys
        at all, so the receipt comes back <code>na</code> instead of <code>pass</code>,
        and only inclusion and the anchor proof are checked. <code>--json</code> gives
        machine-readable output, and it is the only place the anchor status, height and
        confirmations we recorded appear, under <code>reported</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Claims -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="claims-heading">
      <h2 id="claims-heading">5. What we claim, and what we don't</h2>
      <p class="body-text">
        Anchor status moves <code>pending</code> to <code>confirmed</code> to
        <code>final</code>. Every bundle is served at whatever stage it has reached and
        labelled honestly, so you choose the threshold your use case needs.
      </p>
      <ul class="claims mt-sm">
        <li>
          <strong>Entries are anchored in batches, not individually.</strong> Many
          entries across many accounts share one Merkle root and one OpenTimestamps
          attestation. There is no Bitcoin transaction per entry — anyone claiming
          otherwise is describing something nobody could afford.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Anchor metadata is reported, not trusted.</strong> The verifier never
          presents our recorded status, block height or confirmations as verified. They
          appear only in <code>--json</code> output, under <code>reported</code>, as our
          observation. They are not a trust root, and you should not treat them as one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Matching a commitment to the real chain is outside the offline
          verifier.</strong> That needs a block header source. The verifier checks
          structure and commitment; confirming the block is a step you can take
          independently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A receipt proves we saw the entry, not that it is true.</strong> It
          establishes that this exact content existed at that time and has not changed
          since. Nothing more.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Next -->
    <section class="section cta-section" aria-labelledby="next-heading">
      <h2 id="next-heading">Next</h2>
      <p class="body-text">
        The full endpoint reference, with request and response schemas, is published as
        OpenAPI at <a href="https://api.trustnotch.com/docs">api.trustnotch.com/docs</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="/trustnotch-mcp/" class="btn btn-secondary">MCP server for agents →</a>
        <a href="/how-verification-works/" class="btn btn-secondary">How verification works →</a>
        <a href="/pricing/" class="btn btn-secondary">Plans and pricing →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .hero { padding-block-start: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5rem); }
  .hero__sub {
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 1.1875rem);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    max-width: 58ch;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .subsection-title {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .body-text {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    max-width: 65ch;
  }
  .mt-sm { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  .mt-lg { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .claims {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .claims li {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .claims strong { color: var(--color-text); }
  .cta-section { border-bottom: none; }
  .cta-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
